{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts the nocturnal UHI is 'primarily sustained' by anthropogenic heat and scales 'directly with population density.' This misidentifies the dominant mechanism. On a clear, calm night, the key driver of the nocturnal UHI is the reduced rate of longwave radiative cooling caused by the urban canopy geometry (low sky view factor) and the delayed release of heat stored during the day in urban thermal mass. Anthropogenic heat is generally a secondary contributor. Consequently, cross-city variation is best explained by differences in sky view factor, thermal mass, and impervious cover, not a direct linear scaling with population density.","premise":"The problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains both the nocturnal warming and the cross-city variation in the nighttime gap.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"3130303030303030","quote":"what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others"}],"target":"79b147150fca9ed8879c2e014fbc57ebe94aa004c18a5ea26cb34ff92c327e42"}]}